# Per-leg shards from `tools/size_term_paired_floor`. INTERMEDIATE, and ignored rather than
# committed because everything in them is folded into the artefact beside them -- the witness
# counts, the elapsed times and each leg's own peak RSS all reach
# `value_cycle_size_term_paired_floor.json`, which IS tracked. Their job is to let a family that
# loses a leg to the OOM killer resume from the legs already run instead of paying for all seven
# again, and a resume store is state about one machine's interrupted run, not a record.
docs/observability/value_cycle_size_term_paired_floor_legs/
# The delivery lane's live claim store and its draw ledger. RUNTIME STATE, never tracked: a claim
# is about what is in hand right now, and a committed one would be three lanes' merge conflict.
#
# Ignored explicitly from 2026-09-02, when the reason was that the seat executor wrote a COPY into
# its worktree for the child's `--landed`/`--release` to reach. That reason expired on 2026-09-05:
# `CLAIMS_FILE` now resolves through `seat_continuation.shared_tree_dir()`, so there is one store,
# in the main worktree, and no per-worktree copy is written at all.
#
# THE ENTRY STAYS, AND NOT MERELY OUT OF CAUTION. Being ignored is load-bearing in the main tree
# for the original harm -- a broad pathspec committing one lane's live claims -- and it is why
# `ensure_worktree`'s `git clean -qfd` (no `-x`) never removed a worktree copy, which is the false
# premise `seat_executor.bound_landing` used to rest its "written by THIS turn" leg on. If this
# line is ever dropped, read that docstring first.
docs/observability/.delivery_lane_claims.json
docs/observability/.delivery_lane_claims.draws.json
