# The internal query store (atom G12_queryable_projections). Derived from committed truth and
# REBUILT by tools/build_projections.py on every run, so committing it would create exactly the
# second source of truth the atom exists to prevent -- a divergent copy that outlives its sources.
docs/observability/projections.sqlite
docs/observability/projections.sqlite.rebuilding

# THE HEAD-RED OBSERVATION (2026-09-10). MACHINE STATE, and the module already said so: it splits
# OBSERVATION (`record()`, machine-written, every census run) from ACCEPTANCE
# (`docs/observability/head_red_baseline.json`, "NOTHING WRITES THIS FILE AUTOMATICALLY", written
# by a person). Only the second is a property of the tree, and it stays tracked. Three properties
# make the first machine state, each on its own sufficient:
#   1. a machine writes it, on every run, unattended;
#   2. `del runs[:-MAX_RUNS_KEPT]` -- it deletes its own history to a rolling 30 rows. A file that
#      truncates itself is a cache, not a record;
#   3. it describes ONE machine, at ONE head, on ONE night. A checkout inherits it as established
#      fact, which is exactly how it got authority it never earned.
# THE HARM, measured: the sole committed row of the LEGACY tracked path was the 2026-09-02
# ENOSPC/tmpfs wreck -- 830 red, `OSError x760`, `"passed": null`, a row `record()` would now
# REFUSE for having no pass count. So every isolated worktree, clean HEAD extract and fresh
# checkout read `drawable() == 830` while the shared tree correctly read 43.
# WHY A NEW PATH RATHER THAN `git rm --cached` ON THE OLD ONE, measured before choosing (see
# `background/head_red_register.py`, LEGACY_OBSERVED_PATH): the shared tree holds both legacy
# paths DIRTY, and a commit that deletes a path whose worktree copy is dirty makes `git pull`
# ABORT -- not delete. Every lane's fast-forward would have wedged. Dot-prefixed to match the
# convention every other machine-state file in this directory already follows.
docs/observability/.head_red_observed.json
